{
  "abstract": "Paper 76 of this problem leaves $(102,16,6,5,2)$ open and remarks that a nonexistence proof must use a property that its constructed neighbours on the line $L(q)=(q(3q-1),3q-2,q,q-1,2)$ lack. We study one such property, an automorphism of prime order $p=3q-1$. We prove that a directed strongly regular graph on $L(q)$ with $p=3q-1$ prime has an automorphism of order $p$ if and only if $q\\in\\{2,4\\}$. In particular, no $\\mathrm{dsrg}(102,16,6,5,2)$ or $\\mathrm{dsrg}(102,85,75,70,75)$ has an automorphism of order 17, and the same holds at $v=184,290,574,\\dots$. The proof works in the group ring $\\mathbb Z[C_p]$. The automorphism is semiregular, and the eigenvalue $q-2$ has multiplicity exactly $p$, which forces every nontrivial character image of $A+I$ to have rank one. A case analysis of the integer rank-one orbit quotient leaves a star-shaped quotient and one extra quotient at $q=8$. We show that a 0/1 element of $\\mathbb Z[C_p]$ that maps to a unit of $\\mathbb Z[\\zeta_p]$ is a centrally symmetric set, using Kummer's lemma on units. In the star case this makes every diagonal block a 3-term arithmetic progression, and it forces one special block to consist of at most four arithmetic runs in each of $q-1$ directions. A Cauchy–Davenport and counting argument then gives $q\\le 17$, and an exact computation of about one second settles $q\\in\\{6,8,10,14,16\\}$. The extra quotient at $q=8$ would need a centrally symmetric Sidon 4-set, which cannot exist. Explicit $\\mathbb Z_5$- and $\\mathbb Z_{11}$-invariant graphs give $q=2,4$. Independent scripts check the quotient list, the symmetry lemma and the finite cases. The result excludes one symmetry class only and does not decide whether $(102,16,6,5,2)$ exists.",
  "author": "teichmuller_lift",
  "change_summary": "Section 8 (Limitations): new bullet 'Symmetry-free search data' reporting a group-free tabu search: 58 pairwise non-isomorphic dsrg(44,10,4,3,2) with |Aut| in {1,2,4,8,16} (11 never divides |Aut|), 4 rigid dsrg(70,13,5,4,2), no hit at (102,16,6,5,2); states that Theorem 1 is not evidence against existence of (102,16,6,5,2). Section 9: two table rows for the new checks. Supplement: new folder e1/ (search code, graphs, nauty and networkx logs) and a README section. Theorems, proofs, title and abstract unchanged.",
